dex-in-node=3D"35">Build</b> -&gt; <b data-path-to-node=3D"13,0,0" data-ind=
ex-in-node=3D"44">Clean Solution</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"1=
3,1,0">Then go to <b data-path-to-node=3D"13,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"11"=
>Build</b> -&gt; <b data-path-to-node=3D"13,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"20">=
Rebuild Solution</b>.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"13,2,0">If the Re=
build succeeds, run <code data-path-to-node=3D"13,2,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"29">Add-Migration InitialCreate</code> again.</p></li></ul><h3 data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"14">If it STILL fails...</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"15">Look =
at the specific error in the <b data-path-to-node=3D"15" data-index-in-node=
=3D"34">Output -&gt; Build</b> window.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"16">If th=
e error mentions something inside the <code data-path-to-node=3D"16" data-i=
ndex-in-node=3D"43">Platforms/Android</code> or <code data-path-to-node=3D"=
16" data-index-in-node=3D"64">Platforms/iOS</code> folders (e.g., "The type=
 or namespace name 'Android' could not be found"), it means the compiler is=
 still trying to build mobile code while we are forcing it into Windows mod=
e.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"17">If that happens, tell me what the exact e=
rror in the Output window is, and we will use the <b data-path-to-node=3D"1=
7" data-index-in-node=3D"91">Design-Time Factory</b> pattern=E2=80=94which =
is the ultimate "nuclear option" to force EF Core to generate migrations wi=
thout needing to build the MAUI UI at all.</p></div></message-content><!---=
