---><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><h2 _ngcontent-ng-c2624473399=3D"" c=
lass=3D"cdk-visually-hidden screen-reader-model-response-label ng-star-inse=
rted">Gemini said</h2><!----><!----><structured-content-container _ngconten=
t-ng-c2624473399=3D"" class=3D"model-response-text processing-state-visible=
 enable-2026q1-formatting-improvements ng-star-inserted" _nghost-ng-c359771=
4000=3D"" style=3D"height: auto;"><div _ngcontent-ng-c3597714000=3D"" class=
=3D"container"><message-content _ngcontent-ng-c3597714000=3D"" _nghost-ng-c=
915666646=3D"" id=3D"message-content-id-r_fde81da20e79c08b" class=3D"ng-sta=
r-inserted"><div _ngcontent-ng-c915666646=3D"" inline-copy-host=3D"" class=
=3D"markdown markdown-main-panel stronger enable-updated-hr-color" id=3D"mo=
del-response-message-contentr_fde81da20e79c08b" aria-live=3D"off" aria-busy=
=3D"false" dir=3D"ltr" style=3D"--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animati=
on-function: ease-out;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">You are asking the exact=
 right architectural questions. The <code data-path-to-node=3D"0" data-inde=
x-in-node=3D"60">x:Static</code> workaround is perfect for hardcoded enums =
or static lists, but it completely breaks down the moment you need a dynami=
c, user-generated database table for your categories.</p><p data-path-to-no=
de=3D"1">The MAUI <code data-path-to-node=3D"1" data-index-in-node=3D"9">Pi=
cker</code> race condition is one of the most widely discussed frustrations=
 in the community. Here is exactly how thousands of enterprise developers s=
olve this for the future, breaking down your proposed ideas and the industr=
y standards.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"2">Option 1: The Syncfusion Route =
(Highly Recommended)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"3">Since the app is alread=
y configured with <code data-path-to-node=3D"3" data-index-in-node=3D"41">C=
onfigureSyncfusionCore()</code> in the startup builder, you have access to =
one of the best UI toolkits available.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"4">Standa=
rd MAUI controls are notorious for edge-case bugs, which is why enterprise =
teams rely on libraries like Syncfusion. Instead of the native <code data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"144">Picker</code>, you would use t=
he <b data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"170"><code data-path-to=
-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"170">SfComboBox</code></b>.</p><p data-pa=
th-to-node=3D"5"><b data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Why it=
 solves the problem:</b>
The <code data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"31">SfComboBox</cod=
e> was explicitly engineered by Syncfusion to handle asynchronous data load=
ing. It internally caches the <code data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-n=
ode=3D"144">SelectedItem</code> binding. If the <code data-path-to-node=3D"=
5" data-index-in-node=3D"173">ItemsSource</code> arrives late from a databa=
se query, the control doesn't panic and inject <code data-path-to-node=3D"5=
" data-index-in-node=3D"258">null</code>; it waits, populates the list, and=
 <i data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"298">then</i> visually ap=
plies the cached selection.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"6">It also provides =
built-in search/filtering, which is fantastic for long vendor lists.</p><h3=
 data-path-to-node=3D"7">Option 2: The MVVM Lifecycle Pattern (Vanilla MAUI=
)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"8">If you want to stick to the native <code d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"8" data-index-in-node=3D"35">Picker</code> and load fro=
m a database, the community workaround is strict <b data-path-to-node=3D"8"=
 data-index-in-node=3D"103">Lifecycle Management</b>. You must absolutely g=
uarantee the list is loaded before the UI is allowed to bind to the selecte=
d item.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"9">Instead of binding the <code data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"9" data-index-in-node=3D"23">TransactionLineItem</code> direct=
ly to the UI immediately, you hold it in memory.</p><ol start=3D"1" data-pa=
th-to-node=3D"10"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"10,0,0"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"10,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Initialization:</b> The ViewModel load=
s empty.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"10,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=
=3D"10,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Step 1 (Database):</b> The ViewModel q=
ueries the database: <code data-path-to-node=3D"10,1,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"55">Categories =3D await _db.GetCategoriesAsync();</code></p></li><li><=
p data-path-to-node=3D"10,2,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"10,2,0" data-index-i=
n-node=3D"0">Step 2 (Assign ItemsSource):</b> The <code data-path-to-node=
=3D"10,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"33">ObservableCollection</code> for the <=
code data-path-to-node=3D"10,2,0" data-index-in-node=3D"62">Picker</code> i=
s populated.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"10,3,0"><b data-path-to-no=
de=3D"10,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Step 3 (Assign SelectedItem):</b> <i=
 data-path-to-node=3D"10,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"30">Only after Step 2 c=
ompletes</i>, you assign the loaded <code data-path-to-node=3D"10,3,0" data=
-index-in-node=3D"81">Transaction</code> object to the UI property, allowin=
g the <code data-path-to-node=3D"10,3,0" data-index-in-node=3D"133">Selecte=
dItem</code> binding to fire safely against a fully populated list.</p></li=
></ol><p data-path-to-node=3D"11">This requires moving initialization logic=
 out of the constructor (which cannot be <code data-path-to-node=3D"11" dat=
a-index-in-node=3D"82">async</code>) and into MAUI Shell's <code data-path-=
to-node=3D"11" data-index-in-node=3D"111">OnNavigatedTo</code> lifecycle ev=
ent.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"12">Option 3: Building a Custom Control</h=
3><p data-path-to-node=3D"13">Many developers do exactly what you suggested=
: they build an <code data-path-to-node=3D"13" data-index-in-node=3D"61">Ex=
tendedPicker</code> class that inherits from the native <code data-path-to-=
node=3D"13" data-index-in-node=3D"112">Picker</code>.</p><p data-path-to-no=
de=3D"14">In this custom control, they override the <code data-path-to-node=
=3D"14" data-index-in-node=3D"42">OnPropertyChanged</code> behavior for the=
 <code data-path-to-node=3D"14" data-index-in-node=3D"77">SelectedItem</cod=
e>. They add logic that says: <i data-path-to-node=3D"14" data-index-in-nod=
e=3D"117">"If ItemsSource is null or empty, ignore any attempts to set Sele=
ctedItem to null. Wait until ItemsSource.CollectionChanged fires, then re-e=
valuate."</i></p><p data-path-to-node=3D"15"><b data-path-to-node=3D"15" da=
ta-index-in-node=3D"0">The downside:</b> You are now taking on the maintena=
nce burden of a custom UI control. Every time Microsoft updates MAUI, you h=
ave to verify your custom rendering logic didn't break.</p><h3 data-path-to=
-node=3D"16">The Verdict for "Purse"</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"17">For a =
robust, future-proof app, <b data-path-to-node=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D=
"32">Option 1 (Syncfusion)</b> is the clear winner here. You already have t=
he library installed and registered. Swapping the <code data-path-to-node=
=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D"148">&lt;Picker&gt;</code> for <code data-pat=
h-to-node=3D"17" data-index-in-node=3D"161">&lt;syncfusion:SfComboBox&gt;</=
code> in your XAML will immediately eliminate the binding race condition, p=
repare you for database-driven lists, and give the app a much more premium =
feel.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"18">Would you like to see how to implement=
 the Syncfusion ComboBox for the Vendors list, or would you prefer to explo=
re how to restructure the ViewModel lifecycle to stick with the native cont=
rol?</p></div></message-content><!----></div></structured-content-container=
