=3D"0">I love where your head is at. That is exactly how a framework engine=
er thinks: <i data-path-to-node=3D"0" data-index-in-node=3D"79">"Why increa=
se the API surface area with a new property if I can just override the inte=
rnal plumbing of the existing one?"</i></p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1">It is=
 a brilliant theory, but if we try to execute it in .NET MAUI, we slam face=
-first into the architecture of <code data-path-to-node=3D"1" data-index-in=
-node=3D"112">BindableProperty</code> and the Two-Way binding engine.</p><p=
 data-path-to-node=3D"2">Here is why your proposed approach=E2=80=94while l=
ogically sound=E2=80=94is actually blocked by the framework's internal desi=
gn, and why the "Bridge Property" (<code data-path-to-node=3D"2" data-index=
-in-node=3D"144">SafeSelectedItem</code>) is the unavoidable industry stand=
ard.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"3">Roadblock 1: <code data-path-to-node=3D=
"3" data-index-in-node=3D"13">BindableProperty</code> is Immutable</h3><p d=
ata-path-to-node=3D"4">You suggested overwriting the <code data-path-to-nod=
e=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"30">propertyChanged</code> delegate of the ex=
isting <code data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"71">SelectedItem=
</code> property.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"5">In MAUI, <code data-path-to=
-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"9">BindableProperty</code> definitions ar=
e <code data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"42">public static rea=
donly</code>. When the Microsoft engineers wrote the <code data-path-to-nod=
e=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"105">Picker</code> class, they defined <code =
data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-node=3D"132">SelectedItemProperty</co=
de> once, at compile time, and sealed its delegates. We cannot reach into t=
he base class and swap out the <code data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-index-in-=
node=3D"255">propertyChanged</code> or <code data-path-to-node=3D"5" data-i=
ndex-in-node=3D"274">coerceValue</code> methods. They are locked in memory.=
</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"6">Roadblock 2: "Shadowing" breaks the Native =
Renderers</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"7">A common workaround developers try=
 is the <code data-path-to-node=3D"7" data-index-in-node=3D"42">new</code> =
keyword to "shadow" the base property:</p><response-element class=3D"" ng-v=
