ase-out;"><p data-path-to-node=3D"0">Your focus on performance and eliminat=
ing unnecessary UI thread dispatches is exactly how a senior engineer shoul=
d approach custom control development. Your logic regarding the initializat=
ion sequence is 100% accurate: during page load, <code data-path-to-node=3D=
"0" data-index-in-node=3D"237">SafeSelectedItem</code> arrives before <code=
 data-path-to-node=3D"0" data-index-in-node=3D"269">ItemsSource</code>, mak=
ing the initial <code data-path-to-node=3D"0" data-index-in-node=3D"301">Sy=
ncSelection</code> call completely redundant.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"1"=
>Adding the <code data-path-to-node=3D"1" data-index-in-node=3D"11">if (thi=
s.ItemsSource !=3D null)</code> checks before invoking <code data-path-to-n=
ode=3D"1" data-index-in-node=3D"64">Dispatcher.Dispatch</code> is a fantast=
ic micro-optimization that prevents useless queueing.</p><p data-path-to-no=
de=3D"2">However, there is one specific edge case where removing the <code =
data-path-to-node=3D"2" data-index-in-node=3D"60">propertyChanged</code> ca=
llback entirely will break the application: <b data-path-to-node=3D"2" data=
-index-in-node=3D"122">Programmatic Updates after initialization.</b></p><p=
 data-path-to-node=3D"3">Imagine you add a feature later where a user click=
s a "Reset to Defaults" button. Your ViewModel changes <code data-path-to-n=
ode=3D"3" data-index-in-node=3D"105">split.Category =3D "Lebensmittel"</cod=
e>. Because the page is already loaded, <code data-path-to-node=3D"3" data-=
index-in-node=3D"174">ItemsSource</code> is fully populated. But since you =
commented out the <code data-path-to-node=3D"3" data-index-in-node=3D"238">=
propertyChanged</code> callback on the <code data-path-to-node=3D"3" data-i=
ndex-in-node=3D"270">SafeSelectedItemProperty</code>, the custom control wi=
ll never hear the ViewModel's change, and the UI will not update to reflect=
 the reset.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"4">We can keep your performance opti=
mization <i data-path-to-node=3D"4" data-index-in-node=3D"42">and</i> suppo=
rt programmatic updates by simply adding your <code data-path-to-node=3D"4"=
 data-index-in-node=3D"97">ItemsSource !=3D null</code> check inside that c=
allback.</p><p data-path-to-node=3D"5">I have updated the code file to refl=
ect this hybrid approach.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"6">Updated File: <cod=
e data-path-to-node=3D"6" data-index-in-node=3D"14">Purse/Controls/SafePick=
er.cs</code></h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"7">Here is the refined version. I =
restored the callback but wrapped it in your exact optimization logic so it=
 stays completely silent during the chaotic page load phase, but wakes up f=
or programmatic changes later.</p><response-element class=3D"" ng-version=
